From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/a6xx: ROG5 recover from a GMU that does not answer: no ring writes without hw_init, bounded dump waits

2026-10-01 13:32 (bundle main-k111-d10-261001b): GNOME was stopped while
Steam's shader compilation had the GPU busy. On the next GPU resume phoc's
first submit ran hw_init, the GMU did not acknowledge the GPU_SET OOB
request ("Timeout waiting for GMU OOB set GPU_SET: 0x0"), and the GPU never
came back: phoc and Phosh hung, the phone looked frozen and was
hard-rebooted. Three msm problems turned one unanswered OOB request into a
dead GPU:

1. msm_gpu_submit() ignored msm_gpu_hw_init()'s error and wrote the ring
   of a CP that was never set up. The CP fetched from iova 0: hang IRQ
   "gpu fault ... rb 0000/0fb9 ib1 0/0 ib2 0/0" and "*** gpu fault:
   iova=0 dir=READ type=TRANSLATION source=CP", 66 ms after the OOB
   timeout, with phoc's innocent submit blamed.
2. The stalled SMMU fault started a devcoredump: adreno_fault_handler()
   re-armed fault_coredump_done and waited for gpu->lock in
   msm_gpu_fault_crashstate_capture(). The recover worker held gpu->lock
   and, in a6xx_recover() -> runtime suspend -> a6xx_gmu_shutdown()
   (needs_hw_init still set), asked the dead GMU for GPU_SET again; on the
   timeout a6xx_gmu_set_oob() waits for fault_coredump_done without a
   limit. Each waited for the other for good: no second OOB timeout, no
   further GPU message, every GPU client blocked on gpu->lock. The GMU
   watchdog bite 1.5 s later only queued another recovery behind it.
   (a6xx_hfi_wait_for_msg_interrupt() has the same unbounded wait.)
3. A failed init inside a6xx_recover() was ignored too: the replay wrote
   the rings again.

None of this is fixed upstream (v7.3-rc5, msm-next and msm-fixes of
2026-10-01 still have the unbounded waits and the ignored return value).
Changes:

- a6xx_gmu_set_oob() / a6xx_hfi_wait_for_msg_interrupt(): wait at most
  A6XX_GMU_FAULT_DUMP_WAIT_MS (2 s) for a fault devcoredump, then fail the
  request. The recover worker then force-stops the GMU, the dump gets
  gpu->lock afterwards (the hang already captured a crash state, so it
  returns at once) and the SMMU resumes.
- msm_gpu_submit(): after a failed hw_init the submit is queued and
  counted as usual, but the rings are not touched; gpu->deferred_submits
  is set and the recover worker queued. While it is set every later
  submit is deferred the same way, even if its own hw_init succeeds: a
  later fence written directly would retire the unwritten ones.
- recover_worker(): with needs_hw_init or deferred_submits set the first
  pending submit never ran, so no submit is blamed, no fence is skipped
  and no VM is marked unusable: GMU and GPU are restarted (a6xx_recover:
  forced suspend, cold GMU boot, hw_init) and everything is replayed in
  order, then the hangcheck timer is re-armed for the replayed work. If
  hw_init fails again (also in a recovery from an ordinary hang) the
  rings stay untouched, deferred_submits is set so no later submit
  overtakes the queued ones, and the hangcheck timer (armed while
  submits are pending) queues the next attempt; hangcheck_handler() does
  not read CP registers for progress while submits are deferred or the
  GPU is not initialised. The replay empties submits of closed contexts
  as msm_job_run() does (a deferred one was never written empty).
- Read-only module parameters for user space (rog5-gpu-watchdog), read
  without locks: msm.gpu_init_failures (failed hw_init calls in a row,
  reset by a successful one), msm.gpu_inits_ok and msm.gpu_recoveries
  (monotonic counts, so two samples tell one long incident from two
  short ones) and msm.gpu_recovering (the recover worker runs).
  msm_gpu_hw_init() logs failures (rate limited) and the first success
  after them.

What made the GMU stop answering is not known: it booted (firmware start
and HFI start succeeded), then did not acknowledge GPU_SET and its watchdog
bit 1.5 s later; the first such event in the ~30 boots in the journal. The 0102 corner
offsets were empty (no undervolt), and rog5-gmu-bind only binds an empty
driver (the "rog5-gmu-bind 3d6a000.gmu:" prefix of msm's GMU messages is
just the GMU device's driver name). With this patch such a GMU is
force-stopped and cold-booted like after a GMU fault instead of hanging
the GPU.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.h
@@ -224,6 +224,14 @@
 	/* does gpu need hw_init? */
 	bool needs_hw_init;
 
+	/*
+	 * ROG5: submits were queued without being written to a ring (hw_init
+	 * had failed). Later submits must wait for the recovery that replays
+	 * them in order: a later fence written directly would retire them
+	 * unexecuted. Protected by gpu->lock.
+	 */
+	bool deferred_submits;
+
 	/**
 	 * global_faults: number of GPU hangs not attributed to a particular
 	 * address space
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/msm_gpu.c
@@ -18,6 +18,28 @@
 #include <linux/sched/task.h>
 #include <linux/sched/mm.h>
 #include <linux/utsname.h>
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
+
+/*
+ * ROG5: GPU health for user space (rog5-gpu-watchdog), readable without any
+ * lock. gpu_init_failures counts hardware inits in a row that failed (the GMU
+ * did not answer: CP and ring were never set up); a successful init resets
+ * it. gpu_inits_ok and gpu_recoveries count successful inits and started
+ * recoveries since boot, so two samples tell one long incident from two short
+ * ones. gpu_recovering is set while the recover worker runs.
+ */
+static unsigned int rog5_gpu_init_failures;
+module_param_named(gpu_init_failures, rog5_gpu_init_failures, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(gpu_init_failures, "ROG5: failed GPU hardware inits in a row (read-only)");
+static unsigned int rog5_gpu_inits_ok;
+module_param_named(gpu_inits_ok, rog5_gpu_inits_ok, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(gpu_inits_ok, "ROG5: successful GPU hardware inits since boot (read-only)");
+static unsigned int rog5_gpu_recoveries;
+module_param_named(gpu_recoveries, rog5_gpu_recoveries, uint, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(gpu_recoveries, "ROG5: GPU recoveries started since boot (read-only)");
+static bool rog5_gpu_recovering;
+module_param_named(gpu_recovering, rog5_gpu_recovering, bool, 0444);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(gpu_recovering, "ROG5: a GPU recovery is running (read-only)");
 
 /*
  * Power Management:
@@ -172,6 +194,19 @@
 		gpu->needs_hw_init = false;
 	enable_irq(gpu->irq);
 
+	if (!ret) {
+		WRITE_ONCE(rog5_gpu_inits_ok, rog5_gpu_inits_ok + 1);
+		if (READ_ONCE(rog5_gpu_init_failures)) {
+			DRM_DEV_INFO(gpu->dev->dev, "%s: hardware init succeeded after %u failed\n",
+				     gpu->name, rog5_gpu_init_failures);
+			WRITE_ONCE(rog5_gpu_init_failures, 0);
+		}
+	} else {
+		WRITE_ONCE(rog5_gpu_init_failures, rog5_gpu_init_failures + 1);
+		DRM_DEV_ERROR_RATELIMITED(gpu->dev->dev, "%s: hardware init failed: %d (%u in a row)\n",
+					  gpu->name, ret, rog5_gpu_init_failures);
+	}
+
 	return ret;
 }
 
@@ -461,6 +496,8 @@
 	put_task_struct(task);
 }
 
+static void hangcheck_timer_reset(struct msm_gpu *gpu);
+
 static void recover_worker(struct kthread_work *work)
 {
 	struct msm_gpu *gpu = container_of(work, struct msm_gpu, recover_work);
@@ -474,9 +511,24 @@
 	int i;
 
 	mutex_lock(&gpu->lock);
+	WRITE_ONCE(rog5_gpu_recovering, true);
+	WRITE_ONCE(rog5_gpu_recoveries, rog5_gpu_recoveries + 1);
 
 	DRM_DEV_ERROR(dev->dev, "%s: hangcheck recover!\n", gpu->name);
 
+	/*
+	 * ROG5: the GPU was never set up since it last resumed (hw_init failed:
+	 * the GMU did not answer), or submits wait unwritten for this replay.
+	 * The first pending submit did not run, so none is to blame or to
+	 * skip: restart the GMU and GPU and replay everything in order.
+	 */
+	if (gpu->needs_hw_init || gpu->deferred_submits) {
+		DRM_DEV_ERROR(dev->dev, "%s: submits not run (GPU init failed): restarting GMU and GPU, no submit blamed\n",
+			      gpu->name);
+		pm_runtime_get_sync(&gpu->pdev->dev);
+		goto recover;
+	}
+
 	submit = find_submit(cur_ring, cur_ring->memptrs->fence + 1);
 
 	/*
@@ -554,6 +606,7 @@
 		msm_update_fence(ring->fctx, fence);
 	}
 
+recover:
 	priv->disable_err_irq = false;
 
 	gpu->funcs->recover(gpu);
@@ -562,6 +615,25 @@
 	retire_submits(gpu);
 
 	/*
+	 * ROG5: the GPU did not come back (hw_init failed again): never write
+	 * the rings of an uninitialised CP. Keep the submits queued, defer
+	 * every later one until they are replayed (a later fence written
+	 * directly would retire them unexecuted) and let the hangcheck timer
+	 * start the next attempt (hangcheck_handler).
+	 */
+	if (gpu->needs_hw_init) {
+		DRM_DEV_ERROR_RATELIMITED(dev->dev, "%s: GPU did not come back, retrying\n",
+					  gpu->name);
+		gpu->deferred_submits = true;
+		for (i = 0; i < gpu->nr_rings; i++)
+			if (fence_after(gpu->rb[i]->fctx->last_fence,
+					gpu->rb[i]->memptrs->fence))
+				hangcheck_timer_reset(gpu);
+		goto put;
+	}
+	gpu->deferred_submits = false;
+
+	/*
 	 * Replay all remaining submits starting with highest priority
 	 * ring
 	 */
@@ -573,18 +645,30 @@
 		list_for_each_entry(submit, &ring->submits, node) {
 			/*
 			 * If the submit uses an unusable vm make sure
-			 * we don't actually run it
+			 * we don't actually run it. ROG5: likewise for a
+			 * closed context (msm_job_run() empties those, but a
+			 * deferred submit was never written: keep the rule).
 			 */
-			if (to_msm_vm(submit->vm)->unusable)
+			if (to_msm_vm(submit->vm)->unusable ||
+			    submit->queue->ctx->closed)
 				submit->nr_cmds = 0;
 			gpu->funcs->submit(gpu, submit);
 		}
 		spin_unlock_irqrestore(&ring->submit_lock, flags);
 	}
 
+	/* ROG5: the replayed work needs hangcheck (the failed submit or the
+	 * fault IRQ deleted the timer) */
+	for (i = 0; i < gpu->nr_rings; i++)
+		if (fence_after(gpu->rb[i]->fctx->last_fence,
+				gpu->rb[i]->memptrs->fence))
+			hangcheck_timer_reset(gpu);
+
+put:
 	pm_runtime_put(&gpu->pdev->dev);
 
 out_unlock:
+	WRITE_ONCE(rog5_gpu_recovering, false);
 	mutex_unlock(&gpu->lock);
 
 	msm_gpu_retire(gpu);
@@ -658,6 +742,17 @@
 	struct msm_ringbuffer *ring = gpu->funcs->active_ring(gpu);
 	uint32_t fence = ring->memptrs->fence;
 
+	/*
+	 * ROG5: submits wait for a GPU whose hardware init failed (the GMU did
+	 * not answer): no CP runs, so do not read its registers for progress;
+	 * try the recovery again.
+	 */
+	if (READ_ONCE(gpu->deferred_submits) ||
+	    (READ_ONCE(gpu->needs_hw_init) && READ_ONCE(rog5_gpu_init_failures))) {
+		kthread_queue_work(gpu->worker, &gpu->recover_work);
+		return;
+	}
+
 	if (fence != ring->hangcheck_fence) {
 		/* some progress has been made.. ya! */
 		ring->hangcheck_fence = fence;
@@ -794,12 +889,21 @@
 {
 	struct msm_ringbuffer *ring = submit->ring;
 	unsigned long flags;
+	bool hw_ok;
 
 	WARN_ON(!mutex_is_locked(&gpu->lock));
 
 	pm_runtime_get_sync(&gpu->pdev->dev);
 
-	msm_gpu_hw_init(gpu);
+	/*
+	 * ROG5: the return value was ignored. After a failed init (the GMU did
+	 * not answer the GPU_SET OOB request) the ring was written anyway and
+	 * the uninitialised CP fetched from iova 0 (2026-10-01 13:32: CP
+	 * translation fault at 0, rptr 0, ib1/ib2 0). Queue the submit but
+	 * leave the rings alone; recovery restarts the GMU and replays it, and
+	 * every later submit waits for that replay to keep the order.
+	 */
+	hw_ok = !msm_gpu_hw_init(gpu) && !gpu->deferred_submits;
 
 	submit->seqno = submit->hw_fence->seqno;
 
@@ -822,11 +926,18 @@
 	gpu->active_submits++;
 	mutex_unlock(&gpu->active_lock);
 
-	gpu->funcs->submit(gpu, submit);
-	submit->ring->cur_ctx_seqno = submit->queue->ctx->seqno;
+	if (hw_ok) {
+		gpu->funcs->submit(gpu, submit);
+		submit->ring->cur_ctx_seqno = submit->queue->ctx->seqno;
+	} else {
+		gpu->deferred_submits = true;
+		timer_delete(&gpu->hangcheck_timer);
+		kthread_queue_work(gpu->worker, &gpu->recover_work);
+	}
 
 	pm_runtime_put(&gpu->pdev->dev);
-	hangcheck_timer_reset(gpu);
+	if (hw_ok)
+		hangcheck_timer_reset(gpu);
 }
 
